@meta
id: 10-11
title: Brackets and all
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-paren, obj-word, inner-vs-around
par: 74
minutes: 7
vscode: click before the paren, shift-click after it, delete
open: invoice.provider.ts

@brief
`i(` keeps the brackets. `a(` takes them. That is the whole difference,
and it decides which of two very different edits you are making: changing
what a call is passed, or removing the call.

The `Order` entity has dropped its getters. `getTotal()`, `getShipping()`
and `getTax()` are plain fields now — `total`, `shipping`, `tax` — so
every call site has to lose both the `get` prefix and the empty brackets.

Three lines in this provider. Nothing else moves, and the trailing commas
stay.

@keys
| `a(` | the brackets and everything in them |
| `da(` | delete the call's brackets, empty or not |
| `ab` | the same object, no shift key |
| `ciw` | for the name in front of them |

@hints
Each line needs two edits: the name changes, and the `()` goes away
entirely. They are two different objects.
--
`ciw` handles the name. `da(` handles the brackets — and it works on
empty brackets, where there is no inner text to delete at all.
--
`f.` then `l` lands you on the `g` of the getter, `ciw` renames it, then
`f(` and `da(` removes the brackets. Then `j0` and do it again.

@solution
    3j f. l ciw total <Esc> f( da(
    j 0 f. l ciw shipping <Esc> f( da(
    j 0 f. l ciw tax <Esc> f( da(
    :wq<Enter>

Sixty-two keystrokes for six edits across three lines.

`di(` and `da(` are the pair to have straight before you need them under
pressure. `di(` on `refund(order.id)` leaves `refund()` — the call
survives with no arguments. `da(` leaves `refund` — the arguments and the
brackets are gone and you have an identifier. Neither is more correct;
they are answers to different questions.

The empty-bracket case is the one people are surprised by. `di(` on `()`
fails, because there is nothing inside to delete. `a(` does not need there
to be anything inside — it is defined by the brackets themselves — so
`da(` on an empty pair removes them and is the tidiest way to turn a
method call into a property access.

@start invoice.provider.ts
export class InvoiceProvider {
  public build(order: Order) {
    return {
      total: order.getTotal(),
      shipping: order.getShipping(),
      tax: order.getTax(),
    };
  }
}

@expected invoice.provider.ts
export class InvoiceProvider {
  public build(order: Order) {
    return {
      total: order.total,
      shipping: order.shipping,
      tax: order.tax,
    };
  }
}
